{"name":"Jazz Tune","hex":"#9892a8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jazz","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A musical art form rooted in West African cultural and musical expression and in the African American blues tradition, with diverse influences over time, commonly characterized by blue notes, syncopation, swing, call and response, polyrhythms and improvisation.","Energy, excitement, excitability."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To destroy; to ruin.","To play (jazz music)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jazz"},{"term":"tune","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A melody.","A song, or short musical composition."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Used to show appreciation or approval of a song."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tune"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jazz","blocks":[{"text":"Jazz is a music genre that originated in the African-American communities of New Orleans, Louisiana, in the late 19th and early 20th centuries. Its roots are in blues, ragtime, European harmony, African rhythmic rituals, spirituals, hymns, marches, vaudeville song, and dance music. Since the 1920s Jazz Age, it has been recognized as a major form of musical expression in both traditional and popular music. Jazz is characterized by swing and blue notes, complex chords, call and response vocals, polyrhythms and improvisation."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jazz","wikidata":"Q8341"}],"images":[],"sounds":{"jazz":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/30/En-au-jazz.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/3/30/En-au-jazz.ogg/En-au-jazz.ogg.mp3"}],"tune":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/44/En-uk-tune.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_con